 # Hayai on TachiyomiJ2K
 
-Hayai is rooted directly at TachiyomiJ2K commit `7eea215da1a32b7198aabfc3e94ecd71d76f4df7`. The previous mixed Hayai/Rokku tree is preserved at `legacy/hayai-pre-j2k`; none of its Kotlin code is part of the current baseline.
+Hayai is rooted directly at TachiyomiJ2K commit `8df100d6e616851e329b274964c726dcef0556b6`. The previous mixed Hayai/Rokku tree is preserved at `legacy/hayai-pre-j2k`; none of its Kotlin code is part of the current baseline.
 
 ## Boundaries
 
@@ -40,6 +40,12 @@ On first database open, an idempotent importer validates the legacy schema and r
 
 J2K's existing source-migration screens remain the only migration surface. Their source rows are partitioned by the shared Manga/Novel policy, and the target chooser accepts every discoverable `CatalogueSource`, including local and JavaScript novel sources. The policy does not demote an imported novel when its old source is unavailable: a live novel capability, an exact legacy novel genre, a remembered novel-plugin source ID, or retained quotes, highlights, or chapter statistics is sufficient proof. One adapter call invokes Hayai's `NovelMigrationDataMover` inside J2K's existing transaction after target chapters exist; the mover does not open a nested transaction. Before writing, it builds a one-to-one chapter-number map for every chapter carrying selected J2K chapter state or Hayai statistics/highlights and refuses missing or ambiguous mappings. The existing Chapters flag continues to govern J2K read, bookmark, history, `last_page_read`, and `pages_left` state. Hayai quotes, word counts, and highlights always transfer with the novel. Replace preserves row IDs, rebinds quote/highlight ownership, and either rebinds a statistic to the target chapter or removes its source row when the target already has the same count. Copy retains every source row, inserts quotes/highlights under deterministic destination IDs, and inserts a target statistic only when one is absent. An exact pre-existing deterministic copy is an idempotent no-op; a different row at that ID advances through a bounded deterministic identity sequence and aborts if no identity is available. Any different target statistic aborts the caller's transaction, and migration never uses conflict-ignore. Target-owned rows remain intact. Source metadata, repositories, plugin identity, the legacy archive, and offline payloads remain under their original ownership and are not relabelled during migration.
 
+The migration presentation uses `NovelContentIdentity` as the same classifier already consumed by library, details, statistics, and target filtering. Migration rows and flags choose localized manga or novel vocabulary from that identity. `SourceArtwork` supplies one shared icon binding for Browse and migration, while `SourceCoverRequestProvider` lets a JavaScript novel source provide J2K's cover fetcher with the same authenticated client, cookie, and referer it uses for chapter traffic.
+
+Download and backup preferences keep J2K's existing settings and workers, but all location access crosses Hayai's `StorageLocationAccess`. New installations default to app-owned external directories and fall back to internal app storage. User-selected directories retain Android Storage Access Framework write permission, and each use validates that permission and writability before work starts. Raw shared-storage paths remain readable only when Android's all-files permission actually applies. Automatic backup failures use J2K's existing backup notification instead of failing silently. No existing user path is silently moved or rewritten.
+
+Paginated Web layout uses one viewport-wide stride for geometry, seeking, visible-page reporting, and navigation. Offline asset names retain a bounded original extension so WebView can infer CSS, script, font, and image types, while the hash remains the identity. Packaged chapter assets always resolve locally, and unrelated remote custom-style assets remain available when network media is allowed.
+
 ## Reader and source architecture
 
 Extensions remain standard J2K `Source`/`SourceFactory` extensions. `SourceCapabilityRegistry` adds behavior by stable source ID or recognized family name instead of scattering source checks through presenters.
